                <pb facs="0842" n="716" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg716"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5045/254: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-11-25T22:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 25,
                                1925—10 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 25—5:25
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>498. Your 324, November 24, 7 p.m. I am given to understand by senior minister
                    that the British and Japanese judges in their reports confine themselves to
                    closely narrowed findings of facts based upon the evidence as they understand it
                    and keep within the limits of the terms of reference and that the general
                    purport of both reports is that the Shanghai authorities are free from blame.
                    The report of the Japanese judge was received by the senior minister last
                    night.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
